1.1 Using the library
=====================

This section describes how to compile and link programs to use the
Tensor library.  A prerequisite to using Tensor 0.1 in your own programs
is having an installation of GSL (*note (gsl-ref)Obtaining GSL::)
available on your system.

* Menu:

* An example program::          A short example.
* Compiling and linking::       How to compile and link programs.
* Shared libraries::            Using shared libraries.


File: tensor.info,  Node: An example program,  Next: Compiling and linking,  Up: Using the library

1.1.1 An example program
------------------------

The following short program sums the elements of a tensor and displays
the result.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <tensor/tensor.h>

int
main (void)
{
  tensor * t = tensor_alloc (3, 2, 3, 4);

  tensor_set_one (t);

  printf ("Element's sum: %g\n", tensor_sum (t));

  tensor_free (t);

  return 0;
}

   Since all of the tensor's elements are initialised to one, the sum of
elements equals their total number, as shown in the output below:

Element's sum: 24


