1.1.2 Compiling and linking
---------------------------

The library header files are installed in their own ‘tensor’ directory.
Therefore, to compile programs as the one listed in the previous
subsection (*note (tensor)An example program::) any preprocessor include
statements have to be provided with the ‘/tensor’ directory prefix, such
as:

#include <tensor/tensor.h>

   If the directory is not installed on the standard search path of your
compiler you will also need to provide its location to the preprocessor
as a command line flag.  The default location of the ‘tensor’ directory
is ‘/usr/local/include/tensor’.  A typical compilation command for a
source file ‘example.c’ with the GNU C compiler ‘gcc’ is:

$ gcc -Wall -I/usr/local/include -c example.c

   This results in an object file ‘example.o’.  The default include path
for ‘gcc’ searches ‘/usr/local/include’ automatically so the ‘-I’ option
can actually be omitted when Tensor is installed in its default
location.

   The library is installed as a single file, ‘libtensor.a’.  A shared
version of the library ‘libtensor.so’ is also installed on systems that
support shared libraries.  The default location of these files is
‘/usr/local/lib’.  If this directory is not on the standard search path
of your linker you will also need to provide its location as a command
line flag.

   To link against the library you need to specify the Tensor library,
GSL, and a supporting CBLAS library, which provides standard basic
linear algebra subroutines.  A suitable CBLAS implementation is provided
with GSL-in the library ‘libgslcblas.a’, in case your system does not
provide one.  The following example shows how to link an application
with the library:

$ gcc -L/usr/local/lib example.o -lgsl -lgslcblas -lm -ltensor

   The default library path for gcc searches ‘/usr/local/lib’
automatically so the ‘-L’ option can be omitted when Tensor and GSL are
installed in their default location.  The option ‘-lm’ links with the
system math library (not needed on MacOS X).


