1.2 Tensor types
================

The ‘tensor’ structure contains six components: ‘dim’, ‘size’, ‘stride’,
‘data’, ‘block’, and ‘owner’, as listed below.

 -- Type: tensor
     typedef struct
     {
       size_t dim;
       size_t * size;
       size_t * stride;
       double * data;
       gsl_block * block;
       int owner;
     } tensor;

   The number of tensor dimensions is ‘dim’.  The ‘size’ and ‘stride’
are pointers to arrays that contain the size and stride for each
individual dimension.  For a given dimension, its size gives the number
of constituting parts for the dimension.  For example, in a
two-dimensional tensor, i.e., a matrix, ‘dim’ is 2 and ‘size[0]’ gives
the number of rows while ‘size[1]’ the number of columns.  The stride
for a dimension is the step-size between the dimension's constituting
parts in physical memory, measured in units of the underlying data type.
It can differ from the corresponding dimension size to allow the use of
sub-tensors.  The ‘data’ field is a pointer to the memory where the
elements of the tensor are stored.

   Tensors use blocks (*note (gsl-ref)Blocks::) as single underlying
type memory storage--the ‘block’ component in the ‘tensor’ structure.
If the tensor owns the block then the ‘owner’ component is set to one
and the block will be deallocated when the tensor is freed.  If the
block is owned by another object then the ‘owner’ field is zero and any
underlying block will not be freed.

   Tensors support each of the standard data types.  The functions that
operate on ‘double’ data type have the prefix ‘tensor’, whereas for
other data types the type is also included in the prefix, e.g.,
‘tensor_int’ for ‘int’ data type.  Not all functions are applicable to
every data type.  A full list of available tensor types is given below.

Tensor                               Type
                                     
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
tensor                               double
                                     
tensor_float                         float
                                     
tensor_long_double                   long double
                                     
tensor_int                           int
                                     
tensor_uint                          unsigned int
                                     
tensor_long                          long
                                     
tensor_ulong                         unsigned long
                                     
tensor_short                         short
                                     
tensor_ushort                        unsigned short
                                     
tensor_char                          char
                                     
tensor_uchar                         unsigned char
                                     
tensor_complex                       complex double
                                     
tensor_complex_float                 complex float
                                     
tensor_complex_long_double           complex long double
                                     

   The functions for tensor creation and processing are defined in
‘tensor.h’.


