1.5 Tensor initialisation
=========================

The functions listed below initialise the elements of a tensor to
predefined values.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_zero (tensor * t)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to zero.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_one (tensor * t)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to one.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_all (tensor * t, const double x)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to value ‘x’.

   It is possible to initialise a tensor with values drawn from random
number distributions.  All of the following functions have an input
argument ‘rng’, which is a pointer to a GSL random number generator
‘const gsl_rng *’ used for drawing the samples.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_uniform (tensor * t, const gsl_rng * rng)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to random numbers drawn
     from a uniform distribution in the interval ‘[0, 1)’.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_uniform_int (tensor * t, const gsl_rng *
          rng, unsigned long int max)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to random numbers drawn
     from a uniform distribution in the interval ‘[0, max - 1]’.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_normal (tensor * t, const gsl_rng * rng,
          double mu, double sigma)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to random numbers drawn
     from a Gaussian distribution with mean ‘mu’ and standard deviation
     ‘sigma’.

 -- Function: void tensor_set_std_normal (tensor * t, const gsl_rng *
          rng)

     This function sets all tensor ‘t’ elements to random numbers drawn
     from a Gaussian distribution with mean ‘0.0’ and standard deviation
     ‘1.0’.


