1.6 Reading and writing tensors
===============================

 -- Function: int tensor_fread (FILE * stream, tensor * t)

     This function reads into the tensor ‘t’ from the open stream
     ‘stream’ in binary format.  The tensor ‘t’ must be preallocated
     with the correct total size since the function uses the size of ‘t’
     to determine how many bytes to read.  The return value is
     ‘GSL_SUCCESS’ for success and ‘GSL_EFAILED’ if there was a problem
     reading from the file.  The data is assumed to have been written in
     the native binary format on the same architecture.

 -- Function: int tensor_fwrite (FILE * stream, const tensor * t)

     This function writes the elements of the tensor ‘t’ to the stream
     ‘stream’ in binary format.  The return value is ‘GSL_SUCCESS’ for
     success and ‘GSL_EFAILED’ if there was a problem writing to the
     file.  Since the data is written in the native binary format it may
     not be portable between different architectures.

 -- Function: int tensor_fscanf (FILE * stream, tensor * t)

     This function reads formatted data from the stream ‘stream’ into
     the tensor ‘t’.  The tensor ‘t’ must be preallocated with the
     correct total size since the function uses the size of ‘t’ to
     determine how many numbers to read.  The function returns
     ‘GSL_SUCCESS’ for success and ‘GSL_EFAILED’ if there was a problem
     reading from the file.

 -- Function: int tensor_fprintf (FILE * stream, const tensor * t, const
          char * format)

     This function writes the elements of the tensor ‘t’ line-by-line to
     the stream ‘stream’ using the format specifier ‘format’, which
     should be one of the ‘%g’, ‘%e’ or ‘%f’ formats for floating-point
     numbers and ‘%d’ for integers.  The function returns ‘GSL_SUCCESS’
     for success and ‘GSL_EFAILED’ if there was a problem writing to the
     file.


