1.8.1 Basic operators
---------------------

 -- Function: tensor * tensor_copy (const tensor * t_in)

     This function returns a deep copy of tensor ‘t_in’.  After the
     operation both tensor elements are independent, i.e., changes to
     one of the tensors do not reflect in the other.  The copied tensor
     is contiguous even if ‘t_in’ is discontiguous.

 -- Function: int tensor_assign (tensor * t1, const tensor * t2)

     This function assigns all elements from tensor ‘t2’ to tensor ‘t1’.
     After the operation, tensor ‘t1’'s elements have equal values to
     those of tensor ‘t2’.  The function returns ‘GSL_SUCCESS’ if the
     operation was successful, or an error ‘GSL_EBADLEN’ if the total
     number of elements differs between the two tensors.

 -- Function: int tensor_swap (tensor * t1, tensor * t2)

     This function swaps all elements between tensors ‘t1’ and ‘t2’.

 -- Function: void tensor_adjoin (tensor * t)

     This function adjoins a tensor ‘t’, i.e., it makes its underlying
     memory contiguous.  The function has no effect if ‘t’ had already
     been a contiguous tensor.


File: tensor.info,  Node: Shape operators,  Next: Arithmetic operators,  Prev: Basic operators,  Up: Tensor operations

1.8.2 Shape operators
---------------------

The following functions manipulate the shape (dimensions and/or sizes)
of tensors.

 -- Function: int tensor_morph (tensor * t, ...)

     This function changes the sizes of tensor ‘t’ to new sizes provided
     in the variable argument list.  The function returns ‘GSL_SUCCESS’
     if the operation was successful, or ‘GSL_EINVAL’ if the total
     number of elements with the new sizes does not equal the one before
     the operation.  The tensor is adjoined if it was not contiguous.

 -- Function: void tensor_deflate (tensor * t)

     This function removes dimensions of size ‘1’ from tensor ‘t’.

 -- Function: int tensor_inflate (tensor * t, const size_t didx)

     This function adds a dimension of size ‘1’ to tensor ‘t’ at
     dimension ‘didx’.  The function returns ‘GSL_SUCCESS’ if the
     operation was successful, or ‘GSL_EINVAL’ if ‘didx’ is outside the
     range ‘[0, dim]’, where ‘dim’ is the dimension of ‘t’.

 -- Function: tensor * tensor_concat (const size_t didx, const size_t n,
          ...)

     This function returns a tensor which is a concatenation along the
     dimension ‘didx’ of the ‘n’ tensors provided in the variable
     argument list.  The sizes of the input tensors have to match,
     except at the concatenating dimension.


File: tensor.info,  Node: Arithmetic operators,  Next: Trigonometric operators,  Prev: Shape operators,  Up: Tensor operations

1.8.3 Arithmetic operators
--------------------------

 -- Function: void tensor_abs (tensor * t)

     This function works inplace on tensor ‘t’ to provide the absolute
     value of each tensor element.  In case ‘t’ is complex, then the
     magnitude of each element is stored in the real part and the
     imaginary part is set to ‘0’.

 -- Function: void tensor_neg (tensor * t)

     This function works inplace on tensor ‘t’ to provide the negative
     value of each tensor element.  If the tensor contains complex data
     types, it provides the complex negative.

 -- Function: void tensor_inv (tensor * t)

     This function works inplace on tensor ‘t’.  It calculates the
     reciprocal value of each element if the tensor is of a real data
     type, or the complex inverse for complex data types.

 -- Function: int tensor_add (tensor * t1, const tensor * t2)

     This function adds the elements of tensor ‘t2’ to the elements of
     tensor ‘t1’.  The result ‘t1 = t1 + t2’ is stored in ‘t1’ and ‘t2’
     remains unchanged.  The two tensors must have matching sizes.

 -- Function: int tensor_sub (tensor * t1, const tensor * t2)

     This function subtracts the elements of tensor ‘t2’ from the
     elements of tensor ‘t1’.  The result ‘t1 = t1 - t2’ is stored in
     ‘t1’ and ‘t2’ remains unchanged.  The two tensors must have
     matching sizes.

 -- Function: int tensor_mul (tensor * t1, const tensor * t2)

     This function multiplies the elements of tensor ‘t2’ to the
     elements of tensor ‘t1’.  The result ‘t1 = t1 * t2’ is stored in
     ‘t1’ and ‘t2’ remains unchanged.  The two tensors must have
     matching sizes.

 -- Function: int tensor_div (tensor * t1, const tensor * t2)

     This function divides the elements of tensor ‘t2’ from the elements
     of tensor ‘t1’.  The result ‘t1 = t1 / t2’ is stored in ‘t1’ and
     ‘t2’ remains unchanged.  The two tensors must have matching sizes.

 -- Function: int tensor_int_mod (tensor * t1, const tensor * t2)

     This function is defined only for integer data.  It calculates the
     modulus operator or the remainder after division of the elements of
     tensor ‘t2’ from the elements of tensor ‘t1’.  The result ‘t1 = t1
     % t2’ is stored in ‘t1’ and ‘t2’ remains unchanged.  The two
     tensors must have matching sizes.

 -- Function: void tensor_scale_add (tensor * t, const double s, const
          double a)

     This function works inplace to scale the elements of tensor ‘t’ by
     a constant factor ‘s’ and to that add a constant ‘a’.  The result
     ‘t = t * s + a’ is stored in ‘t’.

   The following operators are defined only for complex tensors.

 -- Function: void tensor_conj (tensor * t)

     This function works inplace to provide the conjugate of a complex
     data tensor.

 -- Function: void tensor_arg (tensor * t)

     This function works inplace and calculates the argument of a
     complex tensor.  The argument of a complex number is the angle in
     the complex plane between the positive real axis and a line passing
     through zero and the number.  This angle is measured in the usual
     fashion and ranges from ‘-pi’ to ‘pi’.  It is stored in the real
     part of the element while the imaginary part is set to ‘0’.


